def clean(t):return re.sub(r'\s+',' ',html.unescape(re.sub('<[^>]*>','',t))).strip()
def plain(t):return html.unescape(re.sub('<[^>]*>','',t))
def he(ref,*idx):
 t=CACHE[ref]['he']
 for i in idx:t=t[i-1]
 return re.sub('[\u0591-\u05c7]','',clean(t))
def sub(t,start=None,end=None):
 if start:t=t[t.index(start):]
 if end:t=t[:t.index(end)]
 return t.strip()
def local(book,ref,lo,hi):
 row=next(r for r in json.loads((BOOKS/f'{book}.json').read_text())['paras'] if r['r']==ref)
 return re.sub('[\u0591-\u05c7]','',clean(' '.join(x[0] for x in row['p'][lo:hi])))
def add(s,id,pi,ref,h,en,ctx,pages,anchorEnd=None,url=None,readerRef=None):
 rows=json.loads((LIVE/f'{s-1}.json').read_text());r=next(r for r in rows if r['n']==1);p=plain(r['p'][pi][0]);a=p if anchorEnd is None else p[:p.index(anchorEnd)+len(anchorEnd)]
 url=url or 'https://www.sefaria.org/'+ref.replace(' ','_')
 ev={'ref':ref,'url':url,'he':h,'en':en,'translation':'Original English translation prepared directly from the Hebrew or Aramaic source.','coverage':'Complete selected source unit; all qualifications needed for its meaning retained.','heSha256':hashlib.sha256(h.encode()).hexdigest()}
 e={'section':s,'id':id,'rowN':1,'phraseIndex':pi,'heAnchorPrefix':a,'heCharOffset':len(a),'ref':ref,'url':url,'he':h,'en':en,'context':ctx,'sourceEvidence':[ev],'reviewEvidence':{'pdfPages':pages,'preserveExisting':True,'status':'verified','dedup':'Compared all existing native and connected notes in the destination ois; the selected quotation is not already present.','recovery':'Reconstructed from completed editorial review after workspace reset; primary quotation and exact anchor reverified.'}}
 if readerRef:e['readerRef']=readerRef
 else:e['openExternal']=True
 E.append(e)
def pg(n,i):return {'neimosA':n,'integratedA':i}
add(31,'berakhot-short-prayer-on-the-road',0,'Berakhot 3a:12',he('Berakhot 3a',12),
'It was taught: Rabbi Yosei said: Once I was walking on the road and entered one of the ruins of Jerusalem to pray. Eliyahu, remembered for good, came and waited for me at the entrance until I finished my prayer. After I finished my prayer, he said to me, “Peace be upon you, my master.” I said to him, “Peace be upon you, my master and teacher.” He said to me: My son, why did you enter this ruin? I said to him: To pray. He said to me: You should have prayed on the road. I said to him: I was afraid that passersby might interrupt me. He said to me: You should have prayed a short prayer.',
'This is the exchange behind the ois. Rav Tzadok applies the preference for a short, undisturbed prayer to other situations in which sustained concentration is not possible.',pg([96],[139,140]))
add(31,'tanya-left-chamber-of-the-heart',3,'Tanya, Part I; Likkutei Amarim 9:1-2',he('Tanya, Part I; Likkutei Amarim 9',1)+' '+he('Tanya, Part I; Likkutei Amarim 9',2),
'The dwelling place of the animal soul, which derives from kelipat nogah in every person of Israel, is in the heart, in the left chamber, which is full of blood; and it is written, “For the blood is the soul.” Therefore all desires, self-glorification, anger, and similar traits are in the heart. From the heart they spread throughout the body and also rise to the brain in the head, to think about them, dwell upon them, and devise ways of pursuing them—just as the blood has its source in the heart, spreads from the heart to all the limbs, and also rises to the brain in the head.',
'The “left chamber of the heart” is the spiritual description of the animal soul’s activity. It explains the inner source of the distractions that Rav Tzadok calls “passersby.”',pg([96],[139,140]),anchorEnd='שֶׁבַּלֵּב')
add(31,'yosher-divrei-emet-passersby-and-attachment',3,'Yosher Divrei Emet, First Kuntres 19:1',sub(he('Yosher Divrei Emet, First Kuntres 19',1),'ור"ל אותם הנקראים בנים','עוד כתב שם'),
'That is, those called “sons,” who possess a level of neshamah in addition to their nefesh and ruach, are bound in their thought to the blessed Creator. They may therefore go out even into the marketplace at times when they need to: because they are deeply bound to the blessed Creator, passersby will not interrupt their attachment. This is “sons go out with knots”—with attachment to the Creator in their thought. “Sons of kings” means those who have only nefesh and ruach, who are called sons of kings from the side of the kingdom of Heaven. They go out “with bells”: one must not go out idle, but, like a bell that rings and makes a sound, should continually sound forth words of Torah, prayer, and fear of the blessed God. One must not rely on attachment in thought, for passersby will interrupt it, Heaven forbid, since that attachment is weak. “And every person” means that all people are alike in this respect; scarcely anyone is to be found at the level of the “sons who go out with knots.” Rather, when the Sages spoke of “sons who go out with knots,” they spoke of what was usual—that is, of their own condition. They were speaking about themselves, not others. These are the master’s words.',
'The passage uses “passersby” for interruptions of inner attachment. It expressly qualifies the exceptional level at which silent attachment suffices: ordinary people should continue to voice words of Torah, prayer, and reverence.',pg([96],[139,140]))
add(31,'rashi-havineinu-short-prayer',4,'Rashi on Berakhot 3a:12:2',he('Rashi on Berakhot 3a',12,2),
'“A short prayer”—Havineinu; it is explained below in the chapter Tefillat HaShachar, Berakhot 29a.',
'Rashi identifies the short prayer in this particular incident as Havineinu, the condensed form of the middle blessings of the Amidah.',pg([96],[139,140]))
add(32,'yisrael-kedoshim-holiness-in-enjoyment',1,'Yisrael Kedoshim 5:1',local('yisrael-kedoshim','Yisrael Kedoshim 5:1',0,13),
'The essence of complete holiness is reaching this great level: to be able to make holy use of all one’s enjoyments, without intending one’s own benefit or bodily pleasure. Rabbenu HaKadosh said of himself, in Ketubot 104a, that he had not enjoyed [this world] even with his little finger. See Tosafot there: “Delicacies did not enter his body.” Yet Avodah Zarah 11a says that neither radish nor lettuce, and so forth, ever ceased from his table. See Tosafot, beginning “shelo”: these expand the intestines to accommodate many delicacies. In the comment beginning “tznon,” Tosafot raises the difficulty from Ketubot and gives a strained answer, for the expression “their table” implies that he too ate in this manner. Moreover, why would he provide so many delicacies for his household if he did not desire them and considered them inappropriate? And the passage compares him with Antoninus. Rather, the truth is as I received it, and later saw in a certain book: the statement is to be understood literally. Although his table was full of every worldly delicacy, he nevertheless derived no enjoyment from it at all. All his eating was in exceptional holiness, in accordance with his quality of being called “holy.” This is complete holiness: for all one’s actions and enjoyments to be for the sake of Heaven, without feeling any personal enjoyment at all.',
'This parallel defines the exceptional state in which ordinary pleasures are wholly directed toward Heaven. It clarifies why the ois warns a person to suspect that personal desire may still be present in an apparently permissible pursuit.',pg([97,98],[141,142]),url='#/read/Yisrael_Kedoshim_5',readerRef='Yisrael Kedoshim 5:1')
add(32,'shabbat-truth-stands-falsehood-does-not',3,'Shabbat 104a:9',he('Shabbat 104a',9),
'Shin: sheker, falsehood. Tav: emet, truth. Why are the letters of sheker close together, while the letters of emet are far apart? Falsehood is common; truth is not common. And why does falsehood stand on one leg, while truth has a bricklike base? Truth stands; falsehood does not stand.',
'The saying is based on the positions and shapes of the Hebrew letters. “Falsehood has no legs” expresses the Gemara’s conclusion that falsehood cannot endure.',pg([97,98],[142]))
add(32,'vayikra-rabbah-gateway-to-life',5,'Vayikra Rabbah 30:2',sub(he('Vayikra Rabbah 30:2'),end='שבע שמחות, שבענו'),
'Another interpretation of “You shall take for yourselves”: This is what is written, “You will make known to me the path of life, fullness of joys.” David said before the Holy One, blessed be He: Make known to me which gateway opens through to the life of the World to Come. Rabbi Yudan said: The Holy One, blessed be He, said to David: If you need life, you need afflictions, as it is written, “The way of life is the rebukes of discipline.”',
'The Midrash supplies the phrase “a gateway that opens through.” Rav Tzadok uses that image for a direct path into divine service.',pg([98],[142,143]))
ruinTail=sub(he('Berakhot 3a',15),'״מפני חשד״')+' '+' '.join(he('Berakhot 3b',i) for i in range(1,10))
ruinEn='“Because of suspicion”: But should it not be prohibited because of collapse? In a new [ruin]. But should it not be prohibited because of harmful forces? When there are two people. If there are two, there is no suspicion either! When the two are dissolute. “Because of collapse”: But should it not be prohibited because of suspicion and harmful forces? When there are two upright people. “Because of harmful forces”: But should it not be prohibited because of suspicion and collapse? In a new ruin, with two upright people. If there are two, there are no harmful forces either! In their own place, we are concerned about them. Alternatively: It is indeed a case of one person, in a new ruin that stands in the open country. There is no suspicion there, because a woman is not commonly found in the open country; but there is concern about harmful forces.'
add(32,'berakhot-conditions-for-entering-a-ruin',11,'Berakhot 3a:15-3b:9',ruinTail,ruinEn,
'The existing note quotes the three reasons. This continuation gives the qualifications that Rav Tzadok interprets here: a new ruin, two people, upright companions, and the location of harmful forces. The Gemara discusses a literal ruin; the inward application belongs to Rav Tzadok.',pg([97,98],[143,144]))
add(33,'rashi-zeal-begins-in-the-morning',0,'Rashi on Pesachim 4a:6:1',he('Rashi on Pesachim 4a',6,1),
'“Let us search in the morning”—for the zealous act early, as we find concerning circumcision and Avraham’s zeal: “Avraham rose early in the morning.” He did not wait until sunrise, but nevertheless he did not act earlier, at night.',
'Rashi makes explicit the limit that the ois takes as its starting point: ordinary zeal brings the act forward to the morning, without itself requiring action during the night.',pg([99],[144,145]))
add(34,'tosafot-summer-nights-for-sleep',1,'Tosafot on Eruvin 65a:13:1',he('Tosafot on Eruvin 65a',13,1),
'“Only for sleep”—such as the nights of the summer season.',
'Tosafot limits the saying to the short summer nights. This qualification is the basis of Rav Tzadok’s next discussion of increasing nighttime study as the nights grow longer.',pg([100],[145,146]))
add(34,'kometz-halfway-as-completion',3,'Kometz HaMinchah 2:22:1',local('kometz-haminchah','Kometz HaMinchah 2:22:1',31,33),
'The way of our Sages, of blessed memory, is well known: in every matter they reckon up to the halfway point as a beginning, and after the halfway point as completed.',
'This is Rav Tzadok’s parallel formulation of the principle introduced here by “as I wrote elsewhere.” It supplies the general rule without importing the surrounding discussion of stages of purification.',pg([100],[145,146]),url='#/read/Kometz_HaMinchah_2:22',readerRef='Kometz HaMinchah 2:22:1')
add(34,'mishnah-evening-shema-endpoints',4,'Mishnah Berakhot 1:1',he('Mishnah Berakhot 1:1'),
'From when may one recite the Shema in the evening? From the time when the priests enter to eat their terumah until the end of the first watch: these are Rabbi Eliezer’s words. The Sages say: Until midnight. Rabban Gamliel says: Until dawn. It happened that his sons returned from a feast and said to him: We have not recited the Shema. He said to them: If dawn has not risen, you are obligated to recite it. Not only this: in every case in which the Sages said “until midnight,” the mitzvah extends until dawn. The burning of sacrificial fats and limbs may be performed until dawn; and all offerings eaten for one day may be eaten until dawn. If so, why did the Sages say “until midnight”? To distance a person from transgression.',
'The Mishnah gives the three endpoints that the ois compares. Its final explanation distinguishes the underlying time limit from the Sages’ protective deadline.',pg([100,101],[146]))
add(34,'rabbeinu-yonah-going-to-bed-and-sleeping',5,'Rabbeinu Yonah on Berakhot 1a:2-3',he('Rabbeinu Yonah on Berakhot 1a',2)+' '+he('Rabbeinu Yonah on Berakhot 1a',3),
'“Until the end of the first watch”—that is, one third of the night. There are three watches in the night, as it is written, “at the middle watch”: calling it the middle implies that there is one before it and one after it. Rabbi Eliezer holds that “when you lie down” does not mean all the time people are lying in their beds, but all the time during which they go to lie down. Until a third of the night, some people delay going to bed, and it is still called the time of lying down. “The Sages say,” and so forth: The Sages and Rabban Gamliel disagree with Rabbi Eliezer about the end of the time. They interpret “when you lie down” as all the time people are lying in their beds. Even the Sages, who say “until midnight,” do not disagree with Rabban Gamliel about the interpretation of “when you lie down”: they agree that by Torah law its time extends throughout the night. Their disagreement concerns a safeguard. The Sages made a safeguard to distance a person from transgression; Rabban Gamliel did not require that safeguard. Nevertheless, both the Sages and Rabban Gamliel agree that, from the outset, one should recite it immediately at the appearance of the stars. This is evident from the passage below: “So that a person should not come from the field in the evening,” and so forth. Even when he comes from the field before its time, he should not say: Since the time for the Shema has not yet arrived, I will first eat and drink. Instead, he should read Scripture or study until the stars appear, then recite the Shema and pray, and afterward eat. Since we see that even when he arrives before its time they were concerned that he might become negligent, and required him to read or study so that he would pray immediately, we learn that everyone agrees that one should initially recite it as soon as the stars appear. They disagree only after the fact: the Sages hold that, even after the fact, he fulfills his obligation only until midnight, because of the safeguard; Rabban Gamliel holds that, after the fact, he fulfills it throughout the night.',
'Rabbeinu Yonah distinguishes the period when people begin going to bed from the full period when they remain asleep. The passage also preserves the separate requirement to recite the evening Shema promptly at nightfall.',pg([101],[146]))
add(34,'rashi-hashkivenu-near-dawn',7,'Rashi on Berakhot 9a:2:1',he('Rashi on Berakhot 9a',2,1),
'“Provided that he does not say Hashkivenu”—one who recites the nighttime Shema in the early morning, near dawn, should not say Hashkivenu, for it is no longer the time of beginning to lie down, but the time of finishing one’s sleep.',
'Rashi relates Hashkivenu to beginning to go to bed. Tosafot, cited alongside him, questions the timing of this restriction.',pg([101],[146]),anchorEnd='בְּרַשִּׁ"י')
add(34,'tosafot-hashkivenu-before-and-after-dawn',7,'Tosafot on Berakhot 9a:2:1',he('Tosafot on Berakhot 9a',2,1),
'“Provided that he does not say Hashkivenu”—Rashi explains that this refers to Rabbi Shimon ben Yochai’s statement about someone who recites before dawn. It appears to me that at that time it is certainly still a time for lying down, and he may say Hashkivenu. Moreover, the statement should refer to the discussion immediately preceding it. The master of Coucy therefore explains that it refers to the case just discussed, before sunrise. That is why he says that one may not say Hashkivenu: it is no longer the time for lying down, because dawn has already risen, even though it is still before sunrise.',
'Tosafot distinguishes before dawn from the interval between dawn and sunrise. That distinction is needed for Rav Tzadok’s question about people who begin sleeping late in the night.',pg([101],[146]))
add(34,'tosafot-bedtime-cannot-be-divided-too-finely',9,'Tosafot on Berakhot 2b:16:1',he('Tosafot on Berakhot 2b',16,1),
'“If you think that the poor person’s time is earlier, Rabbi Chanina is the same as Rabbi Eliezer”—one cannot say that the poor person’s time precedes the sanctification of the day, or that the sanctification of the day precedes the poor person’s time. It is not reasonable to distinguish so finely among the measures of the time for lying down, within which all these times fall.',
'Tosafot explains why the Gemara cannot resolve the disagreement by drawing still finer distinctions among nearby bedtimes. Rav Tzadok directs the reader to this constraint on defining the beginning of the sleeping period.',pg([101],[146]))
add(35,'berakhot-three-reasons-and-their-differences',1,'Berakhot 3a:15-3b:9',he('Berakhot 3a',15)+' '+' '.join(he('Berakhot 3b',i) for i in range(1,10)),
'The Sages taught: For three reasons one does not enter a ruin: because of suspicion, because of collapse, and because of harmful forces. '+ruinEn,
'This supplies both the “other two reasons” and the practical differences among them to which Rav Tzadok explicitly refers. The conditions concern a literal ruined building.',pg([102],[147,148]))
add(35,'rashi-storing-wood-and-straw',5,'Rashi on Bava Kamma 21a:8:1',he('Rashi on Bava Kamma 21a',8,1),
'“He uses it”—the owner uses it for wood and straw, keeping his wood and straw inside, and this other person went and lived there. There is no concern about Shaia, since the house is in use. But the consideration that “a house that is inhabited remains settled” still applies: storage is not habitation, and the person who lived there did well and benefited the owner.',
'Rashi distinguishes storage, which prevents the destructive force associated with abandonment, from habitation, which also brings care for the building. Rav Tzadok’s statement that wood and straw suffice concerns the first of these benefits.',pg([102],[147,148]))
add(35,'rashi-shaia-and-maintaining-a-house',7,'Rashi on Bava Kamma 21a:7:1-2',he('Rashi on Bava Kamma 21a',7,1)+' '+he('Rashi on Bava Kamma 21a',7,2),
'“As it is said, ‘Desolation strikes the gate’”—a harmful spirit named Shaia batters the gate of a house in which no people live; therefore the person who stayed in it benefited its owner. Another explanation: A house that is desolate and alone, without people, has its gate battered: harmful forces batter it. “A house that is inhabited remains settled”—a house occupied by people retains its settled condition, because those living inside see what it needs and repair it.',
'The two explanations distinguish destruction associated with abandonment from the ordinary maintenance supplied by inhabitants. Both explain why occupancy benefits the building itself.',pg([102],[147,148]))
add(35,'netivot-olam-settlement-and-harmful-forces',9,'Netivot Olam, Netiv Hatorah 1:7',he('Netivot Olam, Netiv Hatorah 1',7),
'This means that roads are presumed dangerous, as explained in the commentary on the chapters of Avot. The reason roads are presumed dangerous is that there is no human settlement there. Such a place is removed from the essential world, for the essential world is the settled world, and this lies outside settlement. Therefore destructive forces, which oppose the world, hold sway there, and there is reason to fear that forces outside the order of the world will prevail over a person. Thus the statement says: “One who walks on the road and has no escort.” Certainly, when he has an escort, other people accompany him; because a person is with him, it is considered as though he were within the settled world, and he is not called one who has separated from settlement. But someone without an escort and a connection to settlement—what should he do to gain that connection? He should engage in Torah, for Torah is the order that preserves the world, as explained. When he attaches himself to Torah, he has an escort and a connection to the order of the world. Preserving that order saves him from destructive forces, so that they do not prevail; such forces prevail only when a person separates from the world, as we have said. That is why all roads are presumed dangerous. But when he attaches himself to Torah, he has an escort into the world’s order, and therefore does not leave the settled world. This is a complete escort for a person. If connection to other people is considered an escort—because he is then not regarded as being alone, but connected to the creatures that make up the world—then all the more so when he attaches himself to Torah, which is the order of the whole world and in which everything is found. He has a complete escort, connects himself to the world, and is not separated from it so that destructive forces would prevail over him. This is clear.',
'Maharal explains the opposition between human settlement and destructive forces. The connection to Torah supplies the spiritual counterpart to the protection of a settled, inhabited place.',pg([102],[147,148]))

